 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487259 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d889d998-6d7a-58b2-a490-fd41b8228c67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487291 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+4)^{2}=-3(x-3)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0668c7c6-b240-56da-94fb-07d6ea371e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487291 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-4).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+4=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-3(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-3(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+4=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-1=3t,\\,y+4={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2210e304-f1be-5ab9-a627-e9a4a8410a48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487305 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-1}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y+4$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9} & =y+4 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =9(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487310 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487310 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487310 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487310 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-2,3)$ and which passes through the point $(-10,-5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487310 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f7086f8-f36f-50c7-ad0c-2ef637d5cf6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487310 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,-5),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=-5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-8=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-2)t,\\,y-3=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-4t,\\,y-3=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-323212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-4=3{t}^{2},\\,y+2=6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4ed1fe6-d320-5a32-bfb8-73846e898159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487377 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x-4}{3},\\,t=\\frac{y+2}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+2}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x-4}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{x-4}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =36\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =12(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487378 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c50b5890-601e-5b61-a526-2b2221f83863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487378 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{3},\\,t=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2(y+2)}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{4(x+1)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{3} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{4(x+1)}{3}) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =3(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487386 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487386 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487386 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487386 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(x+5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487386 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da2c9d27-2db1-52e6-a2e3-b28e1798b755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487386 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-5,-2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p(x+5) & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+5) \\\\ 4p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\\,y+2=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{4}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
